{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Add an `mcp` autodeploy target to `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh` implementing the ADR 0079\namendment's blue-green pointer-swap, so the on-box `rebar-mcp` service tracks `origin/main`\nwithout ever killing an in-flight certified op. Single concern: the MCP deploy path in\nautodeploy; the service/edge itself is esok, secrets are avians. No new timer unit is needed —\nthe existing `rebar-autodeploy.timer`→`rebar-autodeploy.service` already runs `autodeploy.sh`,\nwhich now also drives the mcp target.\n\n## Approach + rationale (blue-green, per ADR deft-evolutive-mosasaur / ADR 0079 amendment)\nModel the MCP deploy on the LOCAL `origin/main`-tracking updater (external local prior art\n`~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh`, `sticky-genetic-narwhal`): immutable release + atomic\npointer swap + retire-when-idle — NOT the review-bot stop-and-health-drain. On a detected MCP\nsource change:\n1. Build the new MCP image (`docker compose build mcp`) and tag an immutable release\n   `compose-mcp:<target-sha>`.\n2. **Memory-pressure pre-check BEFORE starting the overlap.** The blue-green window runs 2x\n   `rebar-mcp` on the `t4g.large` (8 GiB) box. Read `MemAvailable` from `/proc/meminfo` (MB);\n   if it is a confident reading below `MCP_MEM_MIN_MB` (default 1024 MB), ABORT the overlap:\n   emit the countable `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_MEM_ABORT` marker (routed to the SNS alerts topic by the\n   pipeline in \"Alerting\" below), record a SHA-keyed backoff, and do NOT start a second\n   container (fail-safe: the old container keeps serving). An unreadable measurement\n   (non-Linux/absent `/proc/meminfo`, override empty) fails OPEN (proceed) — blocking deploys\n   on an unmeasurable box would invent a new freeze. A `MCP_MEM_AVAILABLE_MB` env override makes\n   the branch deterministically testable.\n3. Start a NEW `rebar-mcp` container alongside the old one (one-in/one-out overlap cap; never N\n   parallel). The new container publishes on the OTHER host loopback port of the A/B pair\n   (`MCP_PORT_A=8091`/`MCP_PORT_B=8092`, container port fixed 8091); the current live port is\n   read back from the installed nginx include.\n4. Health-check the NEW container (`/health` 200 within `MCP_HEALTH_TIMEOUT`, default 120s). On\n   failure: discard the new container (`docker rm -f`), leave the OLD nginx upstream include\n   UNCHANGED (rollback = do nothing; old container still serving), emit `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR`\n   (reason `mcp-unhealthy`), record a SHA-keyed backoff, exit non-zero. The next timer tick\n   retries (a transient/slow start succeeds on retry).\n5. **Atomically flip the nginx `/mcp/` upstream** to the new container: back up the current\n   installed include, write `server 127.0.0.1:<newport>;` to a temp file and `mv` it over\n   `/etc/nginx/mcp-upstream.conf` (atomic rename), run `nginx -t`, then `nginx -s reload`. If\n   `nginx -t` or the reload fails, RESTORE the backed-up include (disk and loaded config agree\n   on the OLD upstream), discard the new container, record a backoff, exit non-zero. The deploy\n   is DONE at a successful reload; it NEVER waited on or killed an in-flight op.\n6. **Retire the OLD container off the critical path**, gated by a \"still serving?\" liveness\n   check on its own `/health` `in_flight` gauge (counts only long-running certified LLM ops).\n   Retain the newest `MCP_RELEASES_KEEP` (default 1) old containers as a rollback lifeline;\n   remove older containers only when idle (`in_flight == 0`). NEVER kill a container reporting\n   `in_flight > 0`. If the total count of managed `rebar-mcp-*` containers exceeds\n   `MCP_RELEASES_CAP` (default 3) because busy old containers cannot be reclaimed, emit the\n   countable `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP` marker (SNS-routed) instead of killing a live container\n   or growing unbounded.\n\nWhy: eliminates review-bot bug 7b4a (a never-idle shared MCP server can't satisfy a\ngauge-gated drain bound, so the bound is exhausted and an op is killed; a bigger bound only\ndefers it). Decoupling retirement from deploy means the deploy never blocks and never kills; a\nkilled op is client-visible + idempotent (event-sourced), so retirement is safe.\n\n## Retire-when-idle re-trigger (no new source change required)\n`autodeploy.sh` exits early on the up-to-date path, so a still-serving old container left behind\nby a deploy would otherwise only be revisited on the NEXT mcp source change. To honor\nretire-when-idle, run a lightweight `mcp_retire_sweep` on the up-to-date/no-op tick — the same\nplacement as `reclaim_under_pressure` — so an old container is retired on a later tick once it\ngoes idle, and the over-cap `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP` alert still fires if busy containers\naccumulate. The sweep does no build/flip; it only reclaims idle old containers and alerts over\ncap.\n\n## Change detection + blast radius\nAdd `MCP_PATHS`, matching the canonical set esok committed as the compose-comment contract this\nplan consumes (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` `mcp:` service header):\n`MCP_PATHS='src/rebar infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp infra/compose/docker-compose.yml uv.lock pyproject.toml'`.\nDiffed `deployed..target` it triggers ONLY an `mcp` rebuild. Because `src/rebar/` and\n`docker-compose.yml` are shared with the review-bot's `BOT_PATHS`, a shared change triggers BOTH\ntargets — each is change-detected, drained, and deployed INDEPENDENTLY in its own `if changed`\nblock. `gerrit` is never touched (retain the existing explicit assert on the gerrit container id;\nadd the same before/after gerrit-id assertion across the mcp deploy). `flock` still serializes.\n\n## Failure interaction with the single deployed-sha + backoff (shared-change case)\nThe `mcp` branch failure is FATAL exactly like the `review-bot` branch: it calls\n`record_backoff_failure` and `exit 1`, so the success footer that advances `deployed-sha` is NOT\nreached and the SHA-keyed backoff throttles retries. For a shared `src/rebar/` change that\ntriggers both targets, the review-bot block runs first; if it fails it exits before the mcp\nblock. If the review-bot succeeds but the mcp deploy fails, the mcp block records the backoff and\nexits non-zero, so `deployed-sha` does not advance and BOTH targets are re-attempted on the next\ntick (the review-bot rebuild/up is idempotent). This guarantees a failed mcp deploy is always\nretried.\n\n## Alerting (marker → observability → CloudWatch → SNS; NO direct SNS publish)\nautodeploy.sh has no `sns:Publish` path and must not gain one (no IAM grant on the box). The\n\"SNS alert\" is emitted the SAME way every existing autodeploy alert is: a countable journald\n`marker AUTODEPLOY_MCP_* {json}` on stderr → the host observability probe\n(`infra/scripts/observability.sh`) greps the unit journal via `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta`\ninto a dimensionless `rebar/host` custom metric → a terraform `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm`\nroutes it to `aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn` (the `rebar-gerrit-alerts` topic). This ticket adds:\n- two new tokens `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP` and `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_MEM_ABORT` in autodeploy.sh;\n- two `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` calls (+ their offset files) in observability.sh\n  publishing `rebar/host:mcp_retire_cap` and `rebar/host:mcp_mem_abort`;\n- two `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm` resources (in `infra/terraform/`) wired to\n  `aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn`, mirroring `monitoring_autodeploy.tf`'s dimensionless\n  900s/2-of-4/Sum>0/notBreaching shape.\nBoth markers are distinct tokens from `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR` so they do not inflate the deploy_errors\nalarm, and captured container output (if any) redacts the tokens like `capture_bot_logs` does.\n\n## Portability (blocking `project.portability`)\nThe MCP deploy trigger is the existing on-box `systemd`/`launchd`-style timer polling the git\nmirror — an operation-linked poll, NOT a specific CI provider. No GitHub-Actions-only path; the\ndrain, flip, and retire logic run in-process in the script and work with any/no CI.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Tier: **real subprocess/bash integration with temp git repos + a fake docker/nginx**\n  (test-design §3: \"CLI/env/exit codes\" + \"git refs\"). Tests stub the box's binaries onto PATH\n  and run the REAL `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`, placed with the sibling autodeploy tests in\n  `tests/scripts/`. Oracles, each asserted on real script invocations:\n  - Change detection (a REAL temp git repo with two commits): an MCP-only source change (touch a\n    `MCP_PATHS` file, e.g. `infra/compose/Dockerfile.mcp`, between the two commits) triggers an\n    `mcp` build and NO review-bot/gerrit rebuild; a review-bot-only change (touch a `BOT_PATHS`\n    file NOT in `MCP_PATHS`, e.g. `infra/compose/Dockerfile.reviewbot`) does the inverse; a\n    shared `src/rebar/` change triggers BOTH independently (contrast cases, §6), asserted via the\n    recorded docker call log.\n  - The cutover sequence is start-new → health-check → flip-upstream → retire-old (assert\n    ordering via a recorded call log from a stubbed docker/nginx/curl), and the deploy returns\n    success WITHOUT waiting on `in_flight` (assert: with `in_flight>0` on the OLD container the\n    deploy still completes, flips the include to the new port, and exits 0).\n  - Retire honors liveness: an old container reporting `in_flight>0` is NOT killed (no\n    `docker rm` of it in the call log); over the `MCP_RELEASES_CAP` cap the\n    `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_RETIRE_CAP` marker is emitted (assert the countable token in the journal) and\n    no live container is deleted.\n  - Memory-pressure pre-check: with `MCP_MEM_AVAILABLE_MB` below `MCP_MEM_MIN_MB`, the overlap is\n    ABORTED — no second container is started (no `docker run`/second compose-up in the log), the\n    `AUTODEPLOY_MCP_MEM_ABORT` marker is emitted, a backoff is recorded, and the old upstream is\n    unchanged.\n  - Health-check failure on the new container leaves the OLD upstream include UNCHANGED (rollback\n    oracle: include contents identical to before, old container still serving, `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR`\n    reason `mcp-unhealthy`, backoff recorded, `deployed-sha` not advanced) and a retry on the\n    next healthy tick succeeds and flips the include (§5D).\n  - The `gerrit` container id is asserted UNCHANGED across an mcp deploy (blast-radius oracle),\n    mirroring the existing review-bot blast-radius assert.\n- [ ] observability.sh publishes `rebar/host:mcp_retire_cap` and `rebar/host:mcp_mem_abort` from\n  the two new markers (asserted by the existing observability marker-publish test pattern, or a\n  new sibling test that runs the probe against a seeded journal).\n- [ ] Two terraform CloudWatch alarms route the two new metrics to `aws_sns_topic.alerts.arn`\n  (validated by `terraform fmt`/plan-shaped assertions if a sibling terraform test exists; else\n  the resources mirror `monitoring_autodeploy.tf` exactly).\n\n## Compat / rollback / migration-safety\nAdditive to autodeploy (new independent `if changed \"$MCP_PATHS\"` block + no-op-tick sweep);\nexisting review-bot/gerrit/obs/certbot behavior unchanged (regression oracle: the existing\nautodeploy tests stay green). ADR 0079 amendment + ADR 0104 already document the new target.\nRollback = revert the `mcp` branch of the script + the observability/terraform additions; the\nreview-bot deploy path is independent.\n\nDepends on: deft-evolutive-mosasaur (ADR), cibophobic-holohedral-esok (switchable upstream +\n`/health`)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787623936478480001,"uuid":"74e6f549-122f-4105-88e6-fd241a025779"}